WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.180
Good morning John.

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:05.740
A couple of weeks ago, you and I spent a little bit of time kayaking around a huge lake

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:08.700
that, seemingly impossibly, is in the middle of a desert.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:14.700
And indeed, Lake Powell is not natural. It is the reservoir formed by Glen Canyon Dam.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.900
Now there's a pretty good chance you don't know much about this,

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:22.120
the second largest man-made lake in America, about the tremendous feats of engineering and human

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.500
labor required to get it up and keep it up as well as its fraught environmental legacy,

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.680
contentious past, and limited future.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.480
I didn't really feel like mentioning any of this while we were enjoying it, but

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.880
the fight that lead to the creation of Lake Powell in some ways created the modern environmental movement

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.940
and lead to some aspects of that movement that I don't love.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.600
This marvel of humanity's ability as well as our folly

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.280
is kinda one of my greatest internal debates made real. So, I wanted to talk about that.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:55.980
Up until the 1960's dams were a big win for pretty much everyone, especially in the arid southwest US.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.020
First, beautiful man-made lakes stocked with bass gave fun,

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.920
easy, recreational opportunities to millions of people;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.660
while getting into deep hidden canyons can be all but impossible for a hiker,

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.000
motoring your boat into one is just a few gallons of gas away.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.940
Second, the tremendous water pressure that built up behind tall dams like

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.320
Glen Canyon Dam is also harnessed for energy;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.320
at peak, enough to power two million homes without any carbon dioxide or other emissions.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.180
And most importantly, the Colorado River is a temperamental beast.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:26.060
A lot of water flows through the Colorado River

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.300
but season to season and even year to year

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.340
you never really know if it's going to be there when you need it to flush toilets or irrigate your crops.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.840
If states in the Southwest were going to be able to grow their economies and populations,

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.460
they needed a stable source of water and protection from massive floods

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:45.820
Hoover Dam, of course, created Lake Mead in the 1930's, but a number of other sites for dams became viable

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.720
as technology progressed and the need for water increased. Three sites for large water storage

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.240
included Echo Park, which would have flooded parts of Dinasour National Monument;

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.040
Bridge Canyon, which would've flooded parts of the Grand Canyon; and Glen Canyon.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:03.480
The fight between the Sierra club and the department of the interior of these dams is stuff of environmental legend, and

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.700
I encourage you to read about this era - it's fascinating - but in the end, Bridge Canyon and Echo Park

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.340
were spared; Lake Powell was the price.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.680
This fight not only spawned much of the modern environmental movement,

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:18.880
it also placed it within a frame that I personally find myself frustrated with sometimes:

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.000
one of nature vs. humans and our technology.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:30.300
Like, the fact that we can see these canyons carved by the magnificent, unstoppable power of this tremendously unstable river system

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.000
and think, "Hey, we should control that"

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.720
is simultaneously the height of human effort and the height of human folly.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:44.440
I look at this dam and I think "humans are so freaking amazing and also so freaking stupid."

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:49.560
And indeed these days, much of Lake Powell's water is lost to evaporation - water that would maybe otherwise

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.900
make it to the sea to nourish the Gulf of California's Delta.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.520
The Colorado River almost always runs dry by the time America is done with it now.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.320
It has met the ocean just once in the last twenty years.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.760
And that's just the start of lake Powell's problems.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.300
Fast-flowing rivers carry sediment, but when it hits the reservoir,

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.000
the water slows down and the sediment falls out.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.420
Canyons are now filling with mud, as is the area behind the dam.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.780
In 100-150 years, the sediment will clog the outflow valves

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.200
and there will be no way to generate electricity from the dam.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.800
In 700 years, without some intervention,  the reservoir will completely fill with mud.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.460
These days, drought has brought the reservoir down to just 50% of capacity,

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.180
and there are fears that it will get much worse.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.140
And yet, that's far from the most severe possible problem,

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.680
which is what happens when a dam exceeds 100% of capacity.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.600
We saw this this year when the Orville dam nearly failed when its spillway began to wash away,

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:45.960
and it happened at Glen Canyon in 1983. Due to huge rains, dams upstream dumped so much water

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.780
into Lake Powell that it kept rising even after all valves were opened.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:55.480
The spillway began to wash away, leaving a 30-foot-deep, 80-foot-wide hole in the bedrock of the dam.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:01.080
At times like those, I am reminded of the seemingly innocuous phrase, 'what goes up must come down.'

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:06.280
Someday there will be no dam at Glen Canyon and we can only hope that when that happens,

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:12.420
we do it on purpose. And yet, I kinda love this dam. I love that I can kayak through those canyons.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:16.040
I love that these people have a beach that they can go to, and fish to catch.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:19.800
I often see the environmental movement belittle the kinds of fun these people enjoy:

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:24.240
the jetskis, and the motorboats, and the imagining of nature as something that exists just to serve humanity.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.900
The environmental movement, I think, can go too far into imagining humanity,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:32.880
or at least those willfully ignorant humans, as nothing but a blight on the perfection of nature.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.160
Sometimes, this goes so far as to imagine all technology as a step in the wrong direction,

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.440
as if the only way out of this mess is backward.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.640
But I cannot abide that. I am in love with nature, and I am in love with humans.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:49.300
I won't so far as to say that we are natural, because while we are,

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:53.120
we are also much more, and also, sometimes I think, less.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:59.440
I want Glen Canyon to be a natural, unaltered ecosystem, but I also want people to be able to enjoy Lake Powell.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.840
Part of being human is being able to want contradictory things.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:07.480
But I have been thrilled to watch, especially over the last decade, as technology has come to be seen more as a

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:10.640
tool for protection of the environment than for its destruction.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.940
These large-scale engineering projects that destroyed natural environments

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.620
set up a dichotomy that's now being dismantled by electric cars

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.980
and solar panels and LED light bulbs, but there are still times when I have to

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:28.440
excitedly marvel at these amazing feats of human ingenuity while also kinda wishing they didn't exist.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.400
I disagree with me sometimes, and, especially in this particular moment in history,

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:37.580
when we are all so convinced of our own rightness and righteousness,

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:43.060
I need opportunities to confront, admit, and accept that more than ever.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:44.760
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:49.000
While I was researching this video, I came across two documentary films that the US government made

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:55.720
about Glen Canyon dam: one about the construction and one about the repair after the floods in the 1980's,

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:00.980
and I found them both fascinating to watch and if you are the kind of person who likes to watch those

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:04.700
I've uploaded them on lists to the Vlogbrothers channel and you can do that,

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.920
uh, by clickin' on either of these links here.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:07.760
OKAY BYE

